<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  RUT_TarShallow_FilthAcceptance.xml   UtinniPatches — SUMP_MECHANICS_1 S4

  🔴 Real engine block found this pass, not previously visible anywhere in
  the kit spec or any prior build pass — read directly against the live
  install, not assumed: RM_TarShallow (LIQUID_TYPES_MOD_1's own generated
  Defs/TerrainDefs/RM_Tar.xml, ParentName="WaterShallowBase") inherits
  `filthAcceptanceMask: [None]` UNCHANGED from vanilla's own abstract
  WaterBase (Data/Core/Defs/TerrainDefs/Terrain_Water.xml — checked
  directly, RM_TarShallow overrides neither field). RimWorld/FilthMaker.cs's
  own TerrainAcceptsFilth (read in full) returns false outright whenever
  `terrainDef.filthAcceptanceMask == FilthSourceFlags.None`, before any
  other check runs — so EVERY call to FilthMaker.TryMakeFilth for
  RUT_Filth_MouseTrack on natural tar would silently fail, logging nothing,
  the mechanic's own defining surface producing zero tracks forever. This is
  the actual named ❓ the spec flagged ("verify filth-per-cell caps don't
  erase the pattern at low mouse counts") landing on a DIFFERENT and more
  fundamental block than the one the spec anticipated — see
  RM_CompFilthTrail.cs's own header for the full finding, including why the
  thickness-cap concern itself turned out to be a non-issue.

  A PATCH, not an edit to LIQUID_TYPES_MOD_1's own generated RM_Tar.xml
  ("regenerate only against a re-frozen official dump, never a live
  capture... edit the table, never this file" — that file's own header).
  No MayRequire/FindMod gate against mandrake.rm.flowworks (was
  mandrake.rm.liquidtypes until the 2026-09-16 merge): every other
  Sump-side reference to RM_TarShallow/RM_TarDeep in this item (S1's
  RUT_MoatFusePost.xml conductingTerrains list) already assumes it is a
  live, always-loaded dependency with no gating — matching that precedent
  rather than inventing a new posture. A patch matching nothing logs
  nothing, so this is still a safe no-op on a mod list without liquidtypes.

  RM_TarDeep is deliberately NOT touched here: it is WaterDeepBase-derived,
  Impassable, and a wandering placeholder mouse's own path can never enter
  an Impassable cell — patching it would have no observable effect on this
  mechanic, so it is left alone rather than "fixed" for its own sake.
-->
<Patch>

  <Operation Class="PatchOperationAdd">
    <xpath>/Defs/TerrainDef[defName="RM_TarShallow"]</xpath>
    <value>
      <filthAcceptanceMask>
        <li>Terrain</li>
      </filthAcceptanceMask>
    </value>
  </Operation>

</Patch>
